# VIDE — per-invocation inputs. Copy to `.env` (which is gitignored).
# This is NOT a registry of instances: the set of instances is derived from the
# system (systemd code-server@* units + /etc/vide/*.env), never from this file.
# It holds inputs for a single ./install.sh run, plus a few fleet-scoped settings
# that later `vide` verbs keep reading (the SSO rows below). Never put secrets here.
# Fleet-scoped is NOT the same as re-readable: the SSO parent domain, issuer and
# proxy port are recorded into /etc/vide/sso/fleet.env at the first SSO install
# and read from there afterwards. Changing them here later does not repoint a
# provisioned fleet — deliberately, since one of them is its root of trust.
# This file is root-equivalent, and not only through the rows VIDE reads: EVERY
# key here is injected into the environment (`os.environ.setdefault`) and
# inherited by every child process VIDE runs as root — apt-get, chpasswd,
# visudo, the upstream installers. That is deliberate, so an operator's
# `https_proxy` reaches them, and it means a row like LD_PRELOAD or BASH_ENV
# would too. On top of that, two of the installer URLs below are fetched and
# executed AS ROOT by design (code-server's runs as the instance user).
# VIDE therefore refuses to run when it, or the checkout around it, is
# writable by anyone but root or the operator invoking sudo — and refuses a
# symlinked .env outright, since a link says nothing about who can repoint it.

# Target OS user for this run. Empty = auto-resolve (sudo-invoking user, or the
# dedicated 'vide' user when invoked as bare root).
#VIDE_USER=

# Set to 1 to deliberately run a ROOT instance instead of falling back to 'vide'.
#VIDE_ALLOW_ROOT=0

# Public FQDN for this instance — fills the generated Caddy snippet and enables
# the end-to-end transport probe. Exposure/TLS/whitelist remain YOUR Caddy's job.
#VIDE_FQDN=vide.example.com

# Auth mode for a NEW instance: 'password' | 'sso'. Empty = the wizard asks
# (non-interactive runs default to password). Mode is immutable per instance —
# a stale row that contradicts an existing instance is refused, never applied.
#VIDE_AUTH=

# SSO mode: the shared parent domain the SSO cookie and redirect cover
# (instances live at <sub>.<parent>, the login endpoint at auth.<parent>).
# Persisted fleet-wide at the first SSO install; later runs must agree.
#VIDE_SSO_PARENT_DOMAIN=example.com
# The OAuth client id/secret are NEVER read from this file ("never put secrets
# here" is load-bearing): both arrive per-invocation — the id via --sso-client-id
# or the stdin heredoc, the secret via --sso-secrets-stdin or the wizard's masked
# prompt; never this file, argv, or env.

# Pin a specific code-server version instead of latest (empty = latest).
#VIDE_CODE_SERVER_VERSION=

# Set to 1 to resolve the latest code-server tag at install time and pin THAT, so a
# run is reproducible. Resolved from the /releases/latest redirect (no GitHub API,
# no token). Falls back to unpinned "latest" if resolution fails — never blocks.
#VIDE_CODE_SERVER_PIN_LATEST=0
#VIDE_CODE_SERVER_RELEASES_LATEST_URL=https://github.com/coder/code-server/releases/latest

# Installer URLs — the ONE thing that may break over the years. If any upstream
# moves its install script, override the URL here (one-line fix, no code change)
# and then re-run the verb that READS the row you changed. They are not the same
# verb, and saying otherwise cost a day: `vide toolchain` converges Node and pnpm
# only, so on the day the code-server URL moved, the published one-line fix would
# have exited 0 having done nothing at all.
#   the two toolchain URLs  ->  sudo vide toolchain
#   the code-server URL     ->  sudo vide upgrade <user>   (per instance)
#VIDE_NVM_INSTALLER_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.5/install.sh
#VIDE_PNPM_INSTALLER_URL=https://get.pnpm.io/install.sh
#VIDE_CODE_SERVER_INSTALLER_URL=https://code-server.dev/install.sh

# --- rarely changed ---
#VIDE_PORT_BASE=9797       # first loopback port to try
#VIDE_PORT_MAX=9996        # last loopback port to try (end of the allocation scan)
#VIDE_NODE_MAJOR=26        # minimum Node.js major installed for the workspace
#VIDE_NVM_VERSION=v0.40.5  # pinned nvm installer tag (also feeds the nvm URL above)
#VIDE_DL_RETRIES=3         # download retries on transient faults (4xx fails fast)
#VIDE_DL_RETRY_DELAY=2     # base backoff seconds (linear)
#VIDE_DL_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10
#VIDE_DL_MAX_TIME=300      # per-attempt total ceiling (seconds)
#VIDE_SSO_DIR=/etc/vide/sso   # fleet-scoped SSO state (allowlists, caddy bodies, proxy config)
#VIDE_SSO_PROXY_PORT=4180     # loopback port of the shared oauth2-proxy
#VIDE_SSO_ISSUER_URL=https://accounts.google.com   # OIDC issuer (override doubles as the test seam)
#VIDE_OAUTH2_PROXY_DIR=/opt/vide/oauth2-proxy      # versioned binaries + the 'current' symlink
#VIDE_OAUTH2_PROXY_VERSION=   # pin an oauth2-proxy version (empty = resolve latest at install;
#                             # a pin below the CVE-2026-40575 floor 7.15.2 is refused)
#VIDE_OAUTH2_PROXY_RELEASES_LATEST_URL=https://github.com/oauth2-proxy/oauth2-proxy/releases/latest
#VIDE_OAUTH2_PROXY_DOWNLOAD_BASE=https://github.com/oauth2-proxy/oauth2-proxy/releases/download
# NOTE: there is deliberately no VIDE_ASSUME_YES knob here. Rows in this file are
# parsed by the Python core of BOTH entry points (install.sh and `vide` — neither
# shim shell-sources it), so persisting "skip confirmations" would silently waive
# `vide destroy`/`rotate`'s only guard on every future run. Confirmation-skipping is
# a PER-INVOCATION command-line flag: `vide --yes destroy <user>`. .env is config,
# never control (same reason VIDE_CONFIRM_ROOT is ignored here: process env only).
#VIDE_DRY_RUN=0            # 1 = preview-only converge: print the plan, mutate nothing
#                          # (honored from this file by install.sh's bootstrap too)
#VIDE_DEBUG=0              # 1 = verbose logs (also echoes every mutation: `+ cmd`)
#NO_COLOR=                 # set to disable colored logs
